00:00:51.727 --> 00:00:55.917
- [Voiceover] Joe asks, "Gary, what trends

00:00:55.917 --> 00:00:58.880
should brick and mortar
stores be paying attention to

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:01.510
over the next 12 to 18 months?"

00:01:02.302 --> 00:01:03.579
- Joe, that's a great question.

00:01:03.579 --> 00:01:06.619
You know, for me a lot of
people have been talking about

00:01:06.619 --> 00:01:10.271
the second screen
situation with television.

00:01:10.271 --> 00:01:12.978
People watching TV with their phone.

00:01:12.978 --> 00:01:14.875
And the funny part is they refer to this

00:01:14.875 --> 00:01:15.947
as the second screen.

00:01:15.947 --> 00:01:18.135
I think we're about,
probably about there now,

00:01:18.135 --> 00:01:20.280
but this is very much the first screen

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:22.119
and that's the second screen.

00:01:22.119 --> 00:01:24.137
And we'll get into that
on a different show.

00:01:24.137 --> 00:01:25.465
That was kind of a little gateway drug

00:01:25.465 --> 00:01:26.921
for somebody if they
want to get on the show.

00:01:26.921 --> 00:01:28.873
That would be a good question to ask.

00:01:28.873 --> 00:01:31.371
The trend in retail for me

00:01:31.371 --> 00:01:35.739
is kind of now the second
screen shopping opportunity.

00:01:35.739 --> 00:01:37.134
What I mean by that is,

00:01:37.134 --> 00:01:39.177
this, your eyes, are the first screen,

00:01:39.177 --> 00:01:40.607
what you're actually looking at.

00:01:40.607 --> 00:01:41.782
But think about this.

00:01:41.782 --> 00:01:43.544
One of things that caught
my mind a few months ago

00:01:43.544 --> 00:01:46.405
was I was in a supermarket
and I watched somebody

00:01:46.405 --> 00:01:48.321
go from one aisle to the other,

00:01:48.321 --> 00:01:50.329
and the whole time, she was.

00:01:50.329 --> 00:01:51.443
Sorry, DRock.

00:01:51.443 --> 00:01:54.483
She was shopping and she was doing this

00:01:54.483 --> 00:01:56.283
and she went around the,

00:01:56.283 --> 00:01:57.295
sorry, India,

00:01:57.295 --> 00:01:59.620
and she went around the end cap.

00:01:59.620 --> 00:02:03.119
Now, look, brands pay a crap-load of money

00:02:03.119 --> 00:02:04.626
to get those end caps,

00:02:04.626 --> 00:02:06.643
or to have to have the hottest
product in the world going.

00:02:06.643 --> 00:02:08.626
But usually at big
stores, big supermarkets,

00:02:08.626 --> 00:02:11.272
big-box stores, they're
paying for that positioning

00:02:11.272 --> 00:02:14.082
'cause it's the best position
in the store, those end caps.

00:02:14.082 --> 00:02:17.121
And so the second screen
shopping opportunity

00:02:17.121 --> 00:02:18.870
is really, really fascinating to me.

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:21.726
Geolocated. Beacons in the store.

00:02:21.726 --> 00:02:23.718
You're in the store,
you're shopping about.

00:02:23.718 --> 00:02:25.353
You're getting messaging.

00:02:25.353 --> 00:02:26.110
I mean, there's a lot of people

00:02:26.110 --> 00:02:27.668
who don't want to be
annoyed on their phone,

00:02:27.668 --> 00:02:28.894
but I'm sure plenty of people,

00:02:28.894 --> 00:02:30.988
when they're in Best
Buy or Target or Costco

00:02:30.988 --> 00:02:32.648
or Albertsons or Wal-Mart,

00:02:32.648 --> 00:02:35.432
wouldn't mind getting a quick
little text or notification,

00:02:35.432 --> 00:02:37.603
or, if they're in their Twitter stream,

00:02:37.603 --> 00:02:40.694
they're using that
geolocated data to understand

00:02:40.694 --> 00:02:41.920
to push a tweet.

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:45.112
You're in Wal-Mart; you
get a tweet from Wal-Mart

00:02:45.112 --> 00:02:47.053
that's telling you there's this deal

00:02:47.053 --> 00:02:49.659
and if you click this
button, scan it, Apple Pay.

00:02:49.659 --> 00:02:52.545
Second screen shopping opportunities.

00:02:52.545 --> 00:02:54.409
One of the big things I'm
thinking about for Wine Library

00:02:54.409 --> 00:02:56.852
is I'm kind of getting a little
flirty with the wine world.

00:02:56.852 --> 00:02:58.094
more and more.

00:02:58.094 --> 00:02:59.386
I'm kind of inching back in slow.

00:02:59.386 --> 00:03:00.129
Steve loves it.

00:03:00.129 --> 00:03:03.141
Show Steve's happiness of a face.

00:03:03.141 --> 00:03:04.949
As I'm inching and
thinking more about wine,

00:03:04.949 --> 00:03:06.245
I'm starting to rethink about the store

00:03:06.245 --> 00:03:09.681
and the thought of walking
in and getting content

00:03:09.681 --> 00:03:13.916
and paying for wines across
the board at the store

00:03:13.916 --> 00:03:16.772
at a lower price if you
have the app at register.

00:03:16.772 --> 00:03:19.330
Just, second screen
shopping is a very big deal.

00:03:20.107 --> 00:03:21.493
- [Voiceover] Lisa says,

00:03:21.493 --> 00:03:24.480
"Gary, awesome segment with Seth Meyers."

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:29.171
Lisa asks, "With all this
talk about Snapcash,

00:03:29.171 --> 00:03:32.175
what is the one thing you'll
teach your kids about money?"

00:03:34.095 --> 00:03:35.723
- Thanks for the love, Lisa.

00:03:35.723 --> 00:03:37.023
That's an interesting question.

00:03:37.023 --> 00:03:39.020
You know, my parents taught,

00:03:39.020 --> 00:03:43.473
my mom, actually, really
taught me to respect money.

00:03:43.473 --> 00:03:45.435
You know, my dad, for an immigrant,

00:03:45.435 --> 00:03:48.746
was a big thinker with
money in the business.

00:03:48.746 --> 00:03:52.206
He really let me splurge and take chances.

00:03:52.206 --> 00:03:53.639
I give him a lot of credit for that.

00:03:53.639 --> 00:03:55.782
You know, it's funny. I have a 
weird relationship with money.

00:03:55.782 --> 00:03:56.896
I want it.

00:03:56.896 --> 00:03:59.311
I'm aware of its benefits.

00:03:59.311 --> 00:04:03.677
But I'm much more into the PR legacy.

00:04:03.677 --> 00:04:05.576
You know, where's my place in history.

00:04:05.576 --> 00:04:07.387
Much more so about the dollars.

00:04:07.387 --> 00:04:10.080
I think if you try to
put yourself in history,

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:13.006
the money finds you very quickly.

00:04:13.006 --> 00:04:13.744
You know, I don't know.

00:04:13.744 --> 00:04:16.321
My biggest fear is my
kids are going to be rich

00:04:16.321 --> 00:04:17.831
versus what I grew up with

00:04:17.831 --> 00:04:19.711
and so I'm trying to figure out some level

00:04:19.711 --> 00:04:21.313
of creating respect around it.

00:04:21.313 --> 00:04:23.171
I guess the only thing that's
defaulting into my mind is

00:04:23.171 --> 00:04:25.145
I'm going to make those two work.

00:04:25.145 --> 00:04:26.352
They're going to work

00:04:26.352 --> 00:04:28.001
so they earn their own cash

00:04:28.001 --> 00:04:30.116
and then they can figure out
what their relationship is

00:04:30.116 --> 00:04:31.572
with their cash.

00:04:31.572 --> 00:04:33.007
- Hey GV, it's TF.

00:04:33.007 --> 00:04:34.911
Got a question for you for all my friends

00:04:34.911 --> 00:04:36.931
in the real estate space around the world.

00:04:36.931 --> 00:04:39.202
And the question is, how much

00:04:39.202 --> 00:04:41.942
of my advertising / marketing dollars

00:04:41.942 --> 00:04:45.007
should I be spending on
salespeople, telemarketing efforts,

00:04:45.007 --> 00:04:48.583
versus direct mail, print, traditional,

00:04:48.583 --> 00:04:50.115
versus online.

00:04:50.115 --> 00:04:50.974
You know me, buddy.

00:04:50.974 --> 00:04:54.085
I'm a no-wrong-way-to-generate-leads
kind of guy.

00:04:54.085 --> 00:04:55.283
What's your take on it?

00:04:55.283 --> 00:04:56.505
(person claps)
(people chat)

00:04:56.505 --> 00:05:00.493
- Hey, you know, TF, I
gotta tell you, I agree.

00:05:00.493 --> 00:05:04.255
I mean, obviously I push
new forward ways of thinking

00:05:04.255 --> 00:05:07.598
about selling stuff, whether
it was ecommerce back in '96,

00:05:07.598 --> 00:05:10.895
email marketing in '97,
Google AdWords in 2000,

00:05:10.895 --> 00:05:13.937
banners, then content marketing in 2006.

00:05:13.937 --> 00:05:15.655
I mean, people are talking
about content marketing now.

00:05:15.655 --> 00:05:19.556
I started Wine Library TV
on February 21st, 2006,

00:05:19.556 --> 00:05:22.621
to do content marketing, so
obviously all the social stuff.

00:05:22.621 --> 00:05:24.386
I've got peeps in the background, too.

00:05:24.386 --> 00:05:26.197
You know, I get it.

00:05:26.197 --> 00:05:28.588
Yeah, I think that if you've got a way.

00:05:28.588 --> 00:05:30.655
I know we've talked in
the past that direct mail

00:05:30.655 --> 00:05:32.373
really works for you as a channel.

00:05:32.373 --> 00:05:34.504
Agreed. Do it, if that's working for you.

00:05:34.504 --> 00:05:37.059
I even did direct mail for Wine Library

00:05:37.059 --> 00:05:40.240
seventeen months ago, just to make sure

00:05:40.240 --> 00:05:42.678
it didn't bring any ROI,
and it was a disaster.

00:05:42.678 --> 00:05:45.301
It was scary to me.

00:05:45.301 --> 00:05:47.368
We used to be direct mail juggernauts

00:05:47.368 --> 00:05:50.572
in '98, '99, 2000, where we'd get

00:05:50.572 --> 00:05:52.824
three, four, five, six,
seven percent redemption

00:05:52.824 --> 00:05:53.804
of how many fliers we'd sent out.

00:05:53.804 --> 00:05:55.313
People coming to the store.

00:05:55.313 --> 00:05:58.517
We had six people bring
the coupon to the store

00:05:58.517 --> 00:06:00.152
and we had a big value prop in it.

00:06:00.152 --> 00:06:02.311
So direct mail clearly died for us

00:06:02.311 --> 00:06:04.285
and then other places that have grown.

00:06:04.285 --> 00:06:08.738
And, SEM works, and Facebook
dark posts are working,

00:06:08.738 --> 00:06:10.711
and content clearly has worked.

00:06:10.711 --> 00:06:15.030
So, I'm a no-romance-over-the-lead
kind of guy, as well.

00:06:15.030 --> 00:06:18.072
I mean, here's my thing, though.

00:06:18.072 --> 00:06:20.278
People fall in love with the way

00:06:20.278 --> 00:06:21.903
they've made their money, right,

00:06:21.903 --> 00:06:24.591
because it's working right now.

00:06:24.591 --> 00:06:27.099
I'm thrilled when I think that Instagram

00:06:27.099 --> 00:06:29.462
and Facebook dark posts and Twitter suck.

00:06:29.462 --> 00:06:30.577
Can't wait for that.

00:06:30.577 --> 00:06:34.338
Can't wait for 2024, you know,
when I'm dissing on that.

00:06:34.338 --> 00:06:36.335
I'm like, it's all about
this, the virtual reality.

00:06:36.335 --> 00:06:38.309
Great. Can't wait,

00:06:38.309 --> 00:06:40.546
in the words of Bart Scott.

00:06:40.546 --> 00:06:44.030
And so, I think the biggest thing

00:06:44.030 --> 00:06:46.281
that I get scared about

00:06:46.281 --> 00:06:51.019
is that people get romantic
and don't try new things.

00:06:51.019 --> 00:06:53.317
Every person watching here
should always be spending

00:06:53.317 --> 00:06:55.686
between five to twenty
percent of their money,

00:06:55.686 --> 00:06:57.659
if that's what you've got, or your time,

00:06:57.659 --> 00:07:00.306
if that's what you've got,
on new and innovative things,

00:07:00.306 --> 00:07:02.535
because they need to be prepping for 2016,

00:07:02.535 --> 00:07:04.834
2018, 2022.

00:07:04.834 --> 00:07:07.550
And here's the biggest key, TF.

00:07:07.550 --> 00:07:09.036
No matter what you tell me,

00:07:09.036 --> 00:07:12.310
your direct mail response
and telemarketing response

00:07:12.310 --> 00:07:16.276
is not as good as that
same action 10 years ago.

00:07:16.276 --> 00:07:18.203
If you were doing that same
calling in the background

00:07:18.203 --> 00:07:20.293
and that same direct
marketing 10 years ago,

00:07:20.293 --> 00:07:22.243
it would have had a bigger ROI

00:07:22.243 --> 00:07:26.260
because more people were paying
attention to those channels,

00:07:26.260 --> 00:07:28.536
their actual home phone and their mailbox,

00:07:28.536 --> 00:07:30.556
then they are now in
a world of this, this,

00:07:30.556 --> 00:07:32.204
and everything pulling away.

00:07:32.204 --> 00:07:35.845
Not to mention, the costs
are higher in direct mail

00:07:35.845 --> 00:07:38.121
because, you know, the
post office is subsidizing

00:07:38.121 --> 00:07:39.189
that loss of money.

00:07:39.189 --> 00:07:41.186
So, these are the things
that I think about it.

00:07:41.186 --> 00:07:43.461
It's the arbitrage of
the value of the ROI,

00:07:43.461 --> 00:07:46.108
not necessarily the action itself.

00:07:47.148 --> 00:07:49.202
- [Voiceover] Damian asks,

00:07:49.202 --> 00:07:52.958
"Gary, do you script answers or improvise?

00:07:52.958 --> 00:07:56.820
How much time do you invest in
prep and production per show?"

00:07:56.820 --> 00:07:59.644
- Damian, you know,
here's how it goes down.

00:07:59.644 --> 00:08:03.346
Steve, you know,

00:08:03.346 --> 00:08:05.917
or India in the future might,

00:08:05.917 --> 00:08:08.378
you know, just run through the questions.

00:08:08.378 --> 00:08:10.305
I do want to have once,

00:08:10.305 --> 00:08:11.108
you know, it's funny,

00:08:11.108 --> 00:08:12.687
I just did the Rapid Fire on Bloomberg.

00:08:12.687 --> 00:08:14.033
As a matter of fact, it's
weird that we actually

00:08:14.033 --> 00:08:14.939
even read the questions

00:08:14.939 --> 00:08:17.632
because I actually
prefer them coming cold.

00:08:17.632 --> 00:08:18.561
We may even go to that.

00:08:18.561 --> 00:08:19.861
I'm not sure why I do that.

00:08:19.861 --> 00:08:21.306
But that's it.

00:08:21.306 --> 00:08:23.229
Here come the questions. Cool.

00:08:23.229 --> 00:08:24.228
Maybe gives me a minute or two

00:08:24.228 --> 00:08:25.319
while I'm answering this question

00:08:25.319 --> 00:08:27.441
to think about the next one,

00:08:27.441 --> 00:08:29.981
'cause I want to give the
best answers I possibly can,

00:08:29.981 --> 00:08:32.140
but I'm very comfortable in improv.

00:08:32.140 --> 00:08:33.534
I prefer it.

00:08:33.534 --> 00:08:35.306
I feel like they're fresh.

00:08:35.306 --> 00:08:37.707
I think one of the main reasons
you guys watch this show

00:08:37.707 --> 00:08:40.284
is because I bring it fresh and real,

00:08:40.284 --> 00:08:41.214
and I think that matters,

00:08:41.214 --> 00:08:44.715
and that's pretty much where
I'm at with the prep time.

00:08:44.715 --> 00:08:46.592
And the production,
that's DRock, Staphon,

00:08:46.592 --> 00:08:48.472
new addition.

00:08:48.472 --> 00:08:49.466
He's in the house.

00:08:49.466 --> 00:08:51.115
Loves Kobe; kind of figured that out.

00:08:51.115 --> 00:08:53.297
But I told him the truth, that
I'm starting to love Kobe.

00:08:53.297 --> 00:08:55.173
By the way, when I know
I have a vested interest

00:08:55.173 --> 00:08:58.126
in athletes or teams, I
always refer to the underdog,

00:08:58.126 --> 00:09:01.284
and when I don't have
vested interest in teams,

00:09:01.284 --> 00:09:04.442
besides my two favorite teams
left, the Jets and Knicks,

00:09:04.442 --> 00:09:06.508
if everybody in America loves him or her

00:09:06.508 --> 00:09:08.737
and they're the best,
I go against the grain.

00:09:08.737 --> 00:09:12.069
I hated Tiger Woods
until America hated him.

00:09:12.069 --> 00:09:14.073
I just, I just, I just go,

00:09:14.073 --> 00:09:15.745
I'm just that guy.

00:09:15.745 --> 00:09:17.156
I just, I'm that guy.

00:09:18.526 --> 00:09:20.034
- [Voiceover] Marius asks,

00:09:20.034 --> 00:09:23.583
"Hey Gary, can you explain in more
detail your statement

00:09:23.583 --> 00:09:27.748
from Market Makers that Super
Bowl ads are underpriced?"

00:09:29.169 --> 00:09:30.330
- I sure can, Marius.

00:09:30.330 --> 00:09:31.305
I appreciate the question

00:09:31.305 --> 00:09:33.284
just moments after I got off the set.

00:09:33.284 --> 00:09:34.152
(Gary laughs)
(people laugh)

00:09:34.152 --> 00:09:36.320
You know, I care about attention,

00:09:36.320 --> 00:09:38.758
just like the questions we just answered,

00:09:38.758 --> 00:09:42.287
and so I think, almost
everybody in America,

00:09:42.287 --> 00:09:44.888
when the Super Bowl,
at a Super Bowl party,

00:09:44.888 --> 00:09:47.906
they're watching it, and then
when the commercials comes on

00:09:47.906 --> 00:09:50.691
you hear at parties, (hushes).

00:09:50.691 --> 00:09:55.030
The event is to consume the
commercial at the highest scale.

00:09:55.030 --> 00:09:56.098
Couple of years ago,

00:09:56.098 --> 00:09:59.349
so I did not watch both
Super Bowls that included,

00:09:59.349 --> 00:10:00.505
or no, that's not true.

00:10:00.505 --> 00:10:03.338
The last Super Bowl that the
Patriots and Giants played in,

00:10:03.338 --> 00:10:04.894
I didn't watch it.

00:10:04.894 --> 00:10:07.703
I just laid in my bed, silently.

00:10:07.703 --> 00:10:09.305
And it was surreal,

00:10:09.305 --> 00:10:12.951
because I knew that all of
America was doing one thing,

00:10:12.951 --> 00:10:15.180
and me and AJ were doing another thing,

00:10:15.180 --> 00:10:17.734
which was sitting in silence.

00:10:17.734 --> 00:10:22.494
And so, I remember that really
drills home the fact for me,

00:10:22.494 --> 00:10:25.861
that all of America watches this game

00:10:25.861 --> 00:10:27.973
and then watches those commercials,

00:10:27.973 --> 00:10:30.040
and the attention put on those commercials

00:10:30.040 --> 00:10:32.246
is overwhelming, and
just the bottom line is,

00:10:32.246 --> 00:10:34.289
in that arbitrage of the ROI,

00:10:34.289 --> 00:10:37.447
I truly think that when you
compare a Super Bowl ad,

00:10:37.447 --> 00:10:39.629
four or five million,
compared to other ads,

00:10:39.629 --> 00:10:43.995
hundreds of thousands, that
this return is so much greater

00:10:43.995 --> 00:10:46.456
than hundreds of thousands of
dollars, millions of dollars,

00:10:46.456 --> 00:10:49.335
that people spend on television
during the regular year

00:10:49.335 --> 00:10:51.076
when everybody's DVRing.

00:10:51.076 --> 00:10:53.213
When a commercial comes
on, everybody's in here.

00:10:53.213 --> 00:10:55.720
And so I think it's
underpriced by today's market.

